WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.106 --> 00:00:02.856
(dramatic music)

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.670
- It is time to settle into
that groove into your couch

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.660
and maybe invest in those
surround sound speakers

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.940
that nobody can afford

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.520
because Warner Bros. just announced

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.540
that they're releasing their
entire 2021 slate of movies

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.490
in theaters and on HBO Max.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.460
So what exactly does
this mean for all of us?

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.640
We're gonna break it all down for you

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.130
right now on The Ketchup.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:25.590
So Warner Bros. dropped this bomb

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.970
with the hottest of
sizzle reels last week.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.860
We're talking "The Matrix
4," "Godzilla vs. Kong,"

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:32.220
"Dune," "In The Heights,"

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:35.130
"The Suicide Squad," "Space
Jam: A New Legacy" and more,

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.010
all from the comfort of your own home,

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.670
provided, of course, you
are an HBO Max subscriber,

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.130
the odds of which we're
betting just heavily increased.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:44.550
And fans were already
stoked and frankly shocked

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.050
that "Wonder Woman 1984"

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.550
is coming out on HBO Max on Christmas Day,

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.900
the same time it's coming out in theaters.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.280
Now, the decision to
simulcast big premieres

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.080
is being framed by Warner
Bros. as the best solution

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.970
for an industry that's hemorrhaging money

00:00:57.970 --> 00:00:59.130
from delayed release dates

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.300
and needs to try something
new to keep fans engaged.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.450
One thing worth mentioning:

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.940
While these movies are free to watch

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.360
for HBO Max subscribers,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.610
they are removing any kind
of free trial, and get this:

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:12.570
Each will only be available
for 31 days on HBO Max

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.690
before going to other
video-on-demand rental platforms.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:16.830
You better start charging your friends

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.440
you shared that password with.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:19.500
Just kidding.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.244
- Your username is "password?"

00:01:21.244 --> 00:01:22.283
- It was just easier.

00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:24.290
- Now, all of this sounds
like a good compromise

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:27.490
to audiences. Movie theaters? Not so much.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.250
AMC Theaters is currently
trying to stay afloat themselves

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:33.100
and did not appreciate Warner
offering potential ticket

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.450
buyers access to their upcoming catalog.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.660
Now, AMC does have a history with studios

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.020
trying to cut them out of the deal.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.910
In April, they threatened to
ban all Universal releases

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:42.743
from their theaters

00:01:42.743 --> 00:01:45.480
after "Trolls World Tour" was
released on video-on-demand

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.890
instead of waiting until
theaters were open again.

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.960
But that situation led to a
then unprecedented agreement

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.200
between AMC and Universal to stream films

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.940
after a 14-day window.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.380
So maybe these are just some
hard-bargaining tactics.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:01.350
I mean, AMC is acting
more like ABC, am I right?

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.160
- Always be closing.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.480
- Now Warner and Universal
aren't the only ones to dabble

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:06.800
with at-home releases.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.980
Disney also flirted with a similar model

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:09.813
earlier this year,

00:02:09.813 --> 00:02:11.420
releasing the live-action "Mulan"

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.410
on its own streaming platform, Disney+,

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.390
the same day it came out in theaters,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.350
and will go head-to-head

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:18.700
with "Wonder Woman" on
Christmas with Pixar's "Soul,"

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.150
which, unlike "Mulan," will
be free for subscribers.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.120
You got to think this may
all just be a response

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:24.390
to Christopher Nolan's "Tenet,"

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.040
which came out exclusively
in theaters earlier this fall

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.430
after many delays

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.220
and suffered at the box
office, leaving many Nolan fans

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.450
still wondering what
"Tenet" is even about.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.975
I mean, what really happens
in his movies anyway?

00:02:35.975 --> 00:02:37.995
- Dream within a dream.

00:02:37.995 --> 00:02:39.540
- Hm, I'm impressed.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.180
- Warner Bros. seems to be offering

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.450
the best of both worlds:

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.920
No extra charges beyond
your HBO Max subscription

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.290
and the chance for fans to
see the movies they love

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.890
without worrying for their safety.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.340
You know, like in the
good old days of 2019.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.960
And while no one is exactly thrilled

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:55.380
at what the WB announcement
seems to predict -

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.410
another year of staying
cooped up at home -

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.400
at least one director remains optimistic.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:00.840
When asked about the Warner decision

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.070
during a recent interview,

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.060
"Ocean's 11" director Steven Soderbergh,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.020
who recently signed a deal
to release films on HBO Max,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.820
reminded us not to panic.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.870
"The theatrical business
is not going away.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:12.220
It's all gonna come back...

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.450
because let's be clear:
there is no bonanza

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:15.700
in the entertainment industry

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.770
that is the equivalent
of a movie that grosses

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:20.230
a billion dollars or more theatrically."

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.750
Christopher Nolan also happened
to chime in on the decision,

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.020
calling it "a real bait and switch,"

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.930
which is likely the same sentiment

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.090
as "Dune" and "Godzilla vs.
Kong"'s Legendary pictures,

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.200
who are allegedly
threatening to sue Warner

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.660
over a lack of communication
about the decision to move

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:36.450
their movies to the streaming service.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.124
More on that news as we get it.

00:03:38.124 --> 00:03:40.630
(plane roaring)
- Brace for impact!

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:41.800
Well, let's throw it over to you guys now.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:42.980
what do you think about Warner Bros.'

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:43.900
big announcement?

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.500
Are you planning on getting HBO Max

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.333
if you haven't already,

00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:47.610
or are you holding out to see

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:50.100
Godzilla vs. King Kong on the big screen?

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.540
Let us know all your thoughts and feelings

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:52.410
in the comments section,

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:53.860
and of course, if you want more movie

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:54.890
or streaming news,

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.720
head on over to RottenTomatoes.com.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.530
And if you already know
which of Warner's movies

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.830
are must-sees and which
you plan on skipping harder

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.290
than a scratched Blu-ray,

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.680
let's keep the conversation going

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.350
in our fan group on Facebook.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:06.183
As always, thank you so much

00:04:06.183 --> 00:04:07.360
for watching The Ketchup.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:08.390
My name's Naz Perez,

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.130
and we'll see you in the next video.

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.256
Bye.

00:04:11.256 --> 00:04:13.839
(upbeat music)

